					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/shaggydredlocks" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1722371); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1722371&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1607353014" alt="Shaggydredlocks" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1722371)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/shaggydredlocks" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1722371); return false;" >Shaggydredlocks</a></span> <span class="odate time_1501862741 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">04 Aug 2017 16:05</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-3564905">
									

<p>Disgusting, but I'm with MrWrong in that we could be provided with a stronger visual for the result of this process. I'd recommend cutting down on the second paragraph of the description, and retooling some of that information into a table format, to provide the same level of detail (in its operation) alongside illustrative examples. You may also find a way to sneak in some humor here as well.</p>
<p>Should you go the route of an interview as Roget suggested, you don't need to necessarily need to have some rote explanation of the event leading to his abilities, and if you're considering imparting some of his experience, I'd err away from anything dismal. I would suggest a scene in which a D-class or two are introduced to the agent, and have to put up with testing his foodstuffs. It'd help if the agent was a total jackass about the whole thing, or is otherwise enjoying their disgust.</p>
<p>As-is, its mildly amusing, and definitely unique, but I think you're selling yourself short with just clinical description.</p>
<p>ETA:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>During testing, any windows present must be covered, or otherwise made unavailable for SCP-3307 to see through, in order to limit unintended production of materials.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I see what you're getting at with this, but I think a more sensible option would be to simply stick a bag over its head or something of the sort during transport to and from the testing chamber (why would it have windows to begin with?) or otherwise note caution to prevent line-of-sight with personnel.</p>

